<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Required for fingerprint upload. Without it the SDK reports an UPLOAD_FAILURE naming
         this exact permission (UploadManager.kt:232-242) instead of crashing. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Auto Backup rules are deliberately NOT declared here.

        The SDK's device id lives in the SharedPreferences file MB_device_intel_secure_prefs and
        only survives a reinstall when the host app's Auto Backup rules cover it (see the SDK
        README, "Device ID persistence"). It is tempting to add

            <application android:fullBackupContent="@xml/mb_full_backup_content" ... />

        to this library manifest so it "just works". We do not, for two reasons:

        1. Manifest merger conflict. A host app that already sets android:fullBackupContent or
           android:dataExtractionRules would fail its build with a merger error, because two
           different values for the same attribute cannot be merged. A plugin that breaks the
           client's build on install is worse than a plugin that documents one line.

        2. The rules are include-only, so pointing the host's attribute at our file would RESTRICT
           backup to just the SDK prefs and silently exclude the app's own data.

        The README carries both rule files verbatim, under "Auto Backup (Android)", and tells
        integrators to copy the single <include> line into their own rules (or, if they have no
        rules at all, to change nothing: the platform default backs up all shared prefs
        already). They used to ship here as reference copies under res/xml/, which was a
        mistake: a library's res/ merges into the client's resource table, so
        @xml/mb_full_backup_content became a real, resolvable reference in their namespace -
        making the exact one-liner reason 2 warns against not just tempting but easy.
    -->

</manifest>
